many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism is stoicism a  religion this is an entirely  understandable question and one that I  think is is great to grapple with in the  present time given that we don't have  some sort of entirely agreed-upon  definition of religion and the answer is  that for some people in the present yes  stoicism is for them a religion but in  general no stoicism is not a religion  it's always helpful when considering  anything having to do with stoicism to  look at what the classic representatives  of that school actually had to say now  you can certainly assert that the Stoics  themselves were religious and had some  religious conceptions and much of that  was taking the traditional Greek Roman  religion the spread throughout the  Mediterranean and reinterpreting it in  such a way that it would make sense in  relation to the philosophy they were not  the only people to be doing that by the  way the Platonic school the Aristotelian  school the epicurean school all of these  philosophical schools are reinterpreting  a already their religious framework so  the Stoics do have religious conceptions  but they they didn't have something like  their own religion and as a matter of  fact they would be willing to use the  already existing institutions and  rituals of the religion so for example  you know thinking about temples thinking  about festivals all of that panoply of  religious piety was stuff that could be  adopted for stoicism what we can say  about the Stoics reinterpretation of  traditional  is that it's very rationalistic very  much oriented towards practice the  Stoics did believe that there is a logos  with a capital L rationality pervading  the universe they were perfectly happy  calling this Zeus or God but they  dissociated from the traditional  conception of Zeus as this lightning you  know bolt throwing guy who sleeps around  with a whole bunch of different women  cheating on his wife siring demigods all  over the place getting wrapped up in  arguments between the gods that the  stoic said that's all just fables  when it came to other matters of  religious practice for example Oracle's  we see that the Stoics did think that  there was something to the practice of  divination but that we really shouldn't  make any recourse to it unless you know  it's something that really requires  divine input and in most cases we can  answer the question ourselves and so we  shouldn't really concern ourselves with  divination or what the priests might be  saying so you can say that that stoicism  again was religious and it did in fact  interface with the traditional religion  but it was not a religion in ancient  times how then might stoicism be viewed  as a religion by some modern Stoics well  we don't have an agreed-upon definition  of religion whether we're talking about  in philosophy of religion religious  studies or even more generally anybody  who tries to sell you the notion that  there's one single definition of  religion is peddling snake oil to you so  we have a very broad conception of  religion and people can you know select  certain traits and and call things  religions which may not be acceptable as  such to to other people and some people  have in fact in the present taken  stoicism and used it as something like a  faith very often they're coming out of  other room  this context and seeking for something  some people prefer the term spirituality  rather than religion and that's a bit  broader that can also apply to stoicism  and then there's many many people in the  present who say listen stoicism is a  philosophy or it's a life approach it  has nothing to do with with religion in  the traditional sense  so let's disentangle it and you might  ask well which of these views are the  right view they're all perfectly  legitimate within the present stoicism  you might say as a certain compatibility  to be taken to be a religion this is  when you see people engaging in  particular rituals and and getting  together in communities whether  face-to-face or virtual and getting  tattoos of the stoic flame on on parts  of their body  other people are perfectly content to  say listen it's got nothing to do with  religion so if you had to answer the  question is stoicism and religion you  can say yes it is for some people but  there's no church there's no hierarchy  there's no authoritative body some  people have put together what they call  I think rather facetiously a stoic Bible  but there's really no such thing and the  entire attitude I would say involved in  much of modern stoicism is not quite the  same as what we see in religious  communities so the answer is really  stoicism is not a religion but people  can make it into one if they wish to if  you found this answer helpful for you  then please share it with others who  might benefit from it this work is  entirely supported by crowdfunding on my  patreon site so if you find it valuable  consider becoming a supporter at  patreon.com slash Saddler
 

